17.3.1 Sign Permit Required. Except as specifically excluded from the provisions of this Article, a sign permit is required prior to building, erecting or altering any sign in the City.
17.3.2 Sign Permit Application. A completed sign permit application with all related materials shall be submitted to the City Clerk along with the sign permit application fee as set by the City Council from time to time.
17.3.3 Sign Permit Application Contents. Sign permit applications shall include the following:
(a) The name, mailing address, and telephone number of the applicant;
(b) The physical address of the lot on which the sign will be placed;
(c) An itemized list and a keyed site plan at a suitable scale showing the location of all existing signs and structures currently on the lot or other similar documentation;
(d) An itemized list and a keyed site plan at a suitable scale showing the location of all proposed signs to be located on the lot or other similar documentation;
(e) A scaled drawing with dimensions and specifications, specifying materials, illumination, character sizes, colors, and support systems of each proposed sign;
(f) An estimate of the cost of construction of each proposed sign;
(g) Any other information that may be reasonably required by the Zoning Administrator.
17.3.4 Review of Sign Permit Applications. Sign permit applications shall be reviewed by the Zoning Administrator. The Zoning Administrator shall either grant or deny the sign permit within 14 days of the City Clerk's receipt of the completed sign permit application.
17.3.5 Issuance of Sign Permit. If the application fully complies with the provisions of this Ordinance, applicable City building codes and all other applicable laws, regulations, and ordinances, the Zoning Administrator shall direct the City Clerk to issue the sign permit. In the event the application is denied, the Zoning Administrator shall provide the applicant with a written statement containing the reasons for the denial.
17.3.6 Appeal from Denial of Sign Permit. If the Zoning Administrator denies the application for a sign permit, the applicant may appeal the decision of the Zoning Administrator to the Commission. Applicant's appeal from the denial must be filed, in writing, with the City Clerk within 15 days of applicant's receipt of the Zoning Administrator's written statement containing the reasons for the denial. The Planning and Zoning Commission shall render a decision on the applicant's appeal within 30 days of the City Clerk's receipt of the applicant's written appeal.
17.3.7 Expiration of Sign Permit for Uncompleted Construction. A sign permit shall expire if the sign for which the permit was issued has not been completed within six months of the date of issuance.

