Signs greater than 100 square feet in display area are not permitted in Commercial and Industrial Zoning Districts without a Large Sign Permit. Signs greater than 100 square feet in display area are prohibited in Residential Zoning Districts. The Zoning Administrator shall review all Large Sign Permit applications and shall grant a permit only if the proposed sign meets the safety and aesthetic standards provided below:
(a) Appropriateness. The proposed sign must be conducive to the area;
(b) Obstructions. Said sign shall not obstruct the view of any signs in the immediate vicinity;
(c) Building Size. The building on which the proposed sign is to be placed is at least 4,000 square feet in wall area fronting the sign;
(d) Proportion. Large signs are only appropriate when proportional to the lot size or building size. Proposed large signs must meet at least one but not necessarily all of the following conditions:
(1) Road Frontage. The lot on which the sign will be situated fronts at least 1,000 feet of public roadway; or
(2) Lot Depth. The depth of the lot is at least 200 feet; or
(3) Sign/Building Setback. The proposed sign will be set back no less than 50 feet from the road right-of-way.

